#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:133
msgid ""
"The footprint is the number of contiguous tile elements one invocation owns. "
"Only an `in` scalar travels by value. Everything else is a pointer to the "
"invocation's own elements, and the implementation returns nothing: a return "
"value is stored through the trailing `out` pointer. On the Tile side every "
"parameter, the return slot included, is one argument of the declaration, "
"typed as a tile of the export's size. Slices, raw pointers, tuples, structs, "
"`bool`, generics, `self`, and patterns in parameter position are rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:141
msgid "Rules the tooling enforces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:143
msgid "The attribute reports these at compile time:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:145
msgid ""
"`tile_shape` is present, an integer literal, and positive. A constant or an "
"expression is not accepted: a static library ships one concrete symbol per "
"export."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:148
msgid ""
"The export has an output: a return value, or at least one `&mut` parameter. "
"A declaration's results are its `out` and `inout` arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:150
msgid ""
"The `out` and `inout` parameters form one contiguous run. `fn f(a: &mut f32, "
"b: f32) -> f32` is `[inout, in, out]` and is rejected; write `fn f(b: f32, "
"a: &mut f32) -> f32`, with the `in` parameters first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:153
msgid ""
"Every parameter of one export has the same footprint, because the fragments "
"of one implementation share their invocation count. A `&mut [i32; 4]` next "
"to a `&i32` would need 32 invocations for one argument and 128 for the "
"other, so `fn window(acc: &mut [i32; 4], bias: &i32)` is rejected and `bias: "
"&[i32; 4]` is the accepted form."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:158
msgid ""
"`sync = \"warp\"` needs a tile size that is a multiple of 32 times the "
"footprint, so every invocation fills whole warps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:160
msgid ""
"`name` and `symbol` are ASCII identifiers outside the reserved `cuda_oxide_` "
"prefix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:163
msgid ""
"`cargo oxide tilelib` repeats the same checks when it reads the descriptions "
"back, so a hand-edited description cannot slip past them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:166
msgid "Building the library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:168
msgid "cargo oxide tilelib tilelib_relu --arch sm_120 --print\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:172
msgid ""
"`--arch` is required because the LTOIR inside a library is built for one "
"architecture. In a standalone project, such as one made with `cargo oxide "
"new`, the crate name can be omitted. The command runs `emit-ltoir`, then "
"writes the description modules next to the device artifact:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:177
msgid ""
"tilelib_relu.ltoir               device code for sm_120\n"
"tilelib_relu.tileexports.json    one description per export, written by the "
"compiler\n"
"tilelib_relu.iface.tilebc        declarations, Tile IR bytecode at the "
"assembler's version\n"
"tilelib_relu.impl.tilebc         implementations, same version\n"
"tilelib_relu.tilelib.mlir        readable form of both; --print shows it\n"
"tilelib_relu.tilelib             the library, when tileiras can build it\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:186
msgid ""
"Before writing the modules it checks the emitted NVVM IR: every "
"implementation symbol must be a `define void` with the parameter list the "
"ABI table predicts, for example `define void @relu_impl(float, ptr)`. The "
"assembler never looks inside the code payload, so a missing or misdeclared "
"symbol would otherwise surface only when a cuTile program is compiled "
"against the library. The two bytecode modules are built by the `oxide-"
"tilebc` crate, cuda-oxide's own writer for these two kinds of module; the "
"Tile stack's crates are not involved. writes its own Tile IR with."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:196
msgid "Reading the dump"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:198
msgid ""
"`--print` shows both modules in Tile IR syntax. The text is the same as "
"`tilelib_relu.tilelib.mlir`. For `tilelib_relu`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:201
msgid ""
"// tilelib_relu.iface.tilebc: the interface a cuTile program calls\n"
"cuda_tile.module @tilelib_relu {\n"
"  cuda_tile.preview$func extern @relu(%x: !cuda_tile.tile<128xf32> "
"{cuda_tile.preview$access = #cuda_tile.preview$access<in>}, %relu_out: !"
"cuda_tile.tile<128xf32> {cuda_tile.preview$access = #cuda_tile."
"preview$access<out>}) -> !cuda_tile.tile<128xf32>\n"
"  cuda_tile.preview$func extern @scale_in_place(%x: !cuda_tile.tile<128xf32> "
"{cuda_tile.preview$access = #cuda_tile.preview$access<inout>}, %factor: !"
"cuda_tile.tile<128xf32> {cuda_tile.preview$access = #cuda_tile."
"preview$access<in>}) -> !cuda_tile.tile<128xf32>\n"
"  cuda_tile.preview$func extern @warp_sum(%x: !cuda_tile.tile<128xf32> "
"{cuda_tile.preview$access = #cuda_tile.preview$access<in>}, %warp_sum_out: !"
"cuda_tile.tile<128xf32> {cuda_tile.preview$access = #cuda_tile."
"preview$access<out>}) -> !cuda_tile.tile<128xf32>\n"
"}\n"
"// tilelib_relu.impl.tilebc: the implementations the LTOIR defines\n"
"cuda_tile.module @tilelib_relu {\n"
"  cuda_tile.preview$extern_impl @relu_impl implements @relu(%x: !cuda_tile."
"preview$fragment<1xf32, local, mem_layout = #cuda_tile.preview$layout<():"
"()>, fragmap = #cuda_tile.preview$layout<(128, 1, 1):(1, 0, 0)>> {cuda_tile."
"preview$access = #cuda_tile.preview$access<in>}, %relu_out: !cuda_tile."
"preview$fragment<1xf32, local, mem_layout = #cuda_tile.preview$layout<(1):"
"(1)>, fragmap = #cuda_tile.preview$layout<(128, 1, 1):(1, 0, 0)>> {cuda_tile."
"preview$access = #cuda_tile.preview$access<out>})\n"
"  cuda_tile.preview$extern_impl @scale_in_place_impl implements "
"@scale_in_place(%x: !cuda_tile.preview$fragment<1xf32, local, mem_layout = "
"#cuda_tile.preview$layout<(1):(1)>, fragmap = #cuda_tile."
"preview$layout<(128, 1, 1):(1, 0, 0)>> {cuda_tile.preview$access = "
"#cuda_tile.preview$access<inout>}, %factor: !cuda_tile."
"preview$fragment<1xf32, local, mem_layout = #cuda_tile.preview$layout<():"
"()>, fragmap = #cuda_tile.preview$layout<(128, 1, 1):(1, 0, 0)>> {cuda_tile."
"preview$access = #cuda_tile.preview$access<in>})\n"
"  cuda_tile.preview$extern_impl @warp_sum_impl implements @warp_sum(%x: !"
"cuda_tile.preview$fragment<1xf32, local, mem_layout = #cuda_tile."
"preview$layout<():()>, fragmap = #cuda_tile.preview$layout<(4, 32, 1):(32, "
"1, 0)>> {cuda_tile.preview$access = #cuda_tile.preview$access<in>}, "
"%warp_sum_out: !cuda_tile.preview$fragment<1xf32, local, mem_layout = "
"#cuda_tile.preview$layout<(1):(1)>, fragmap = #cuda_tile.preview$layout<(4, "
"32, 1):(32, 1, 0)>> {cuda_tile.preview$access = #cuda_tile."
"preview$access<out>})\n"
"    synchronization = #cuda_tile.preview$synchronization<warp>\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:217
msgid "How to read it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:219
msgid ""
"The interface has one `preview$func extern` per export. Every Rust parameter "
"is a `tile<128xf32>` argument with its access, and the results are the `out` "
"and `inout` arguments in order. `relu` returns one tile, its `relu_out` slot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:223
msgid ""
"The implementation has one `preview$extern_impl` per export, naming the "
"symbol it `implements`. Every argument is a `fragment`: the piece of the "
"tile one invocation sees. `mem_layout` is the buffer's own layout: `():()` "
"for a by-value scalar, `(1):(1)` for one element behind a pointer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:227
msgid ""
"`fragmap` maps `(invocations, participants, footprint)` onto the tile. "
"`(128, 1, 1):(1, 0, 0)` is one invocation per element, 128 of them. `(4, 32, "
"1):(32, 1, 0)` is 4 invocations of 32 lanes each, which is what "
"`synchronization = warp` requires."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:232
msgid "The summary line names the artifacts and their sizes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:234
msgid ""
"✓ Tile library descriptions written: 3 export(s), Tile IR 13.5 with the "
"preview tag\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:238
msgid "The tileiras requirement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:240
msgid ""
"The descriptions are Tile IR public-preview content, which exists from "
"bytecode 13.5 on. An assembler reads preview content only at the version it "
"calls current, the newest numbered version its `--list-versions` prints, so "
"the command asks the resolved `tileiras` first and writes the descriptions "
"at that version: 13.5 for the CUDA 13.5 assembler, the newer version a newer "
"assembler names, and 13.5 when no assembler can read them yet. `tileiras --"
"preview-create-tile-lib` arrives with CUDA 13.5, the first assembler whose "
"`--list-versions` names 13.5. When the resolved `tileiras` lists 13.5 or "
"newer, the command assembles the interface, the implementations, and the "
"LTOIR into `tilelib_relu.tilelib` (or the path given with `-o`) and prints "
"the `tileiras` invocation it used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:252
msgid "On CUDA 13.4 the command stops after the descriptions and says why:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:254
msgid ""
"Tile library skipped: /usr/local/cuda/bin/tileiras lists bytecode versions "
"13.1, 13.2, 13.3, 13.4; the descriptions need 13.5 or newer\n"
"The installed tileiras cannot create tile libraries: the descriptions are "
"Tile IR public-preview content, which exists from bytecode 13.5 on and needs "
"the CUDA 13.5 or newer assembler.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:259
msgid ""
"It then prints the `tileiras --preview-create-tile-lib` command that turns "
"the written files into the library on a newer toolkit, and exits 2 so a "
"script notices the missing library. `--library-optional` makes that outcome "
"exit 0 when the LTOIR and the descriptions are all you need. Both outcomes "
"print a line a script can match: `Tile library written to` on success, `Tile "
"library skipped:` otherwise. The assembler is resolved from "
"`CUTILE_TILEIRAS_PATH` first, then from `PATH`, the configured CUDA toolkit "
"root, and the standard install locations. The [installation guide](../"
"getting-started/installation.md) covers toolkit selection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:270
msgid "Calling it from cuTile Python"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:272
msgid ""
"A cuTile kernel reaches the export through `cuda.tile_preview.foreign_call`. "
"This is the kernel from the example's `python/call_from_cutile.py`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:275
msgid ""
"import cuda.tile as ct\n"
"from cuda import tile_preview as tp\n"
"\n"
"TILE = 128\n"
"PROVIDER = tp.Tilelib(path=\"tilelib_relu.tilelib\", version=\"0.1.0\")\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def relu_tile(x, y):\n"
"    \"\"\"One block runs `relu` over one 128-float tile of `x` into `y`."
"\"\"\"\n"
"    pid = ct.bid(0)\n"
"    tile = ct.load(x, index=(pid,), shape=(TILE,))\n"
"    # Every declaration argument needs an input tile, the `out` slot "
"included.\n"
"    result_slot = ct.zeros((TILE,), dtype=ct.float32)\n"
"    (out,) = tp.foreign_call(\n"
"        tilelibs=(PROVIDER,),\n"
"        symbol_name=\"relu\",\n"
"        constant_params=(),\n"
"        inputs=(tile, result_slot),\n"
"        output_types=((ct.float32, (TILE,)),),\n"
"    )\n"
"    ct.store(y, index=(pid,), tile=out)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:299
msgid "Three things to get right:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:301
msgid ""
"`symbol_name` is the declaration name, `relu`, not the implementation symbol "
"`relu_impl`. The Tile compiler resolves the call to the implementation when "
"it links the library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:304
msgid ""
"Every declaration argument takes a tile, the `out` ones included. `relu` has "
"two arguments, so `inputs` has two tiles; a zero tile stands in for the "
"result slot. A scalar needs a 0-d tile, and a Python literal is rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:307
msgid ""
"`output_types` lists the results, one `(dtype, shape)` per `out` or `inout` "
"argument in declaration order. `relu` has one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:310
msgid ""
"`foreign_call` reads the library as a compile-time constant, so the "
"`Tilelib` is a module global rather than a kernel argument, and its file has "
"to exist when the kernel is traced. The script's `--dry-run` compiles the "
"kernel to bytecode without `tileiras`, which works on CUDA 13.4 today:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:315
msgid ""
"bytecode: 844 bytes, header 0d050100 (version 13.5, preview flag set)\n"
"PASS: the caller side compiles; the program calls `relu` through "
"preview$call (dry run, no cubin)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:320
msgid ""
"The full run needs the `.tilelib` from a CUDA 13.5 or newer `tileiras` and a "
"cuTile Python that lists that assembler's current bytecode version, because "
"preview content is read only at that version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:324
msgid "Calling it from Tile C++"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:326
msgid ""
"The same toolkit that brings the assembler teaches nvcc `--enable-tile` and "
"`--tile-preview simt`, and a Tile C++ kernel calls the library through a "
"foreign declaration. The example's `cpp/tile_caller.cu` declares all three "
"exports; this is the `relu` one:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:331
msgid ""
"namespace ct = cuda::tiles;\n"
"using tile_f32 = ct::tile<float, ct::shape<128>>;\n"
"\n"
"// @relu(%x: tile<128xf32> in, %relu_out: tile<128xf32> out) -> "
"tile<128xf32>\n"
"__experimental_foreign_decl__ __experimental_foreign_name__(\"relu\")\n"
"    tile_f32 relu(tile_f32 x, [[cutile::experimental_inout]] tile_f32 "
"relu_out);\n"
"\n"
"[[cutile::hint(0, num_worker_warps_per_cta = 4)]] __tile_global__ void "
"kernel(float* x, float* y) {\n"
"    auto xs = ct::partition_view { ct::tensor_span { x, ct::shape<128> {} }, "
"ct::shape<128> {} };\n"
"    auto ys = ct::partition_view { ct::tensor_span { y, ct::shape<128> {} }, "
"ct::shape<128> {} };\n"
"    ys.store(relu(xs.load(0), ys.load(0)), 0);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:346
msgid ""
"A parameter the library reads carries no attribute; one it writes carries "
"`[[cutile::experimental_inout]]` and comes back as the call's result, in "
"argument order. The declaration's name is the library's declaration name, "
"not the implementation symbol. Compile and run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:351
msgid ""
"nvcc --enable-tile --tile-preview simt -Xtileiras=-l=tilelib_relu.tilelib "
"\\\n"
"     -std=c++20 -arch=sm_120 -o tile_caller cpp/tile_caller.cu\n"
"./tile_caller\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:357
msgid ""
"PASS relu: 128 elements through the tile library\n"
"PASS scale_in_place: 128 elements through the tile library\n"
"PASS warp_sum: 128 elements through the tile library, every lane holding its "
"warp's total\n"
"SUCCESS: 3/3 tile exports called from a Tile C++ kernel\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:364
msgid ""
"Because nvcc, tileiras and the Tile C++ front end come from one toolkit, the "
"program and the library always agree on the bytecode version. That is the "
"shortest path to a running Tile kernel on top of a Rust library; the cuTile "
"Python path needs a cuTile Python that lists the same assembler's version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:369
msgid "What happens at the boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:371
msgid ""
"The Tile compiler never hands your function a whole tile. It splits each "
"tile argument into fragments, one per invocation, and calls the "
"implementation once per fragment. With `sync = \"none\"` a fragment is one "
"element, so a 128-element tile means 128 calls, each from one thread. With "
"`sync = \"warp\"` an invocation is a whole warp: 32 lanes call together, "
"each with its own element, and warp intrinsics are legal inside. The calling "
"thread is a real thread of the Tile kernel, and `threadIdx.x` reads the "
"hardware register, so lane and warp identity work as in any SIMT kernel (see "
"the [Execution Model](../gpu-programming/execution-model.md))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:381
msgid ""
"The pointers you receive point at the invocation's own buffer and nothing "
"else. `&mut f32` is one element and `&mut [f32; 4]` is four contiguous "
"elements; the function may not reach past them, and nothing outside the "
"invocation aliases them during the call. The function cannot allocate shared "
"memory, so do not declare a `SharedArray` in an export: the shared memory of "
"a Tile kernel is the Tile compiler's to place, and this release has no way "
"to request a scratch buffer from it (see [Shared Memory and Synchronization]"
"(shared-memory-and-synchronization.md) for what shared memory means in a "
"SIMT kernel). Keep exports to registers, the parameters they receive, and "
"warp traffic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:392
msgid "Not in this release"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:394
msgid ""
"Parametric tile sizes. `tile_shape` is one integer literal, so a library "
"ships one export per tile size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:396
msgid "Warpgroup and CTA scope. `sync` accepts `\"none\"` and `\"warp\"` only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:397
msgid "Scratch memory. An export cannot ask for a shared-memory buffer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:398
msgid "`bf16` parameters. The attribute accepts `f16` but not `bf16` yet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:399
msgid "2-D tiles. `tile_shape` has one extent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:400
msgid ""
"Linkage providers, the form of a library that compiles implementations on "
"demand while the Tile compiler runs. They are not a public CUDA 13.5 "
"surface. A static library ships one implementation per declaration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:403
msgid "A cutile-rs caller. Only cuTile Python has `foreign_call` today."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:405
msgid ""
"The [Supported Features](../appendix/supported-features.md) appendix tracks "
"the interop status alongside the rest of the compiler."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:408
msgid "Verifying the ABI on CUDA 13.4"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:410
msgid ""
"The `.tilelib` needs the CUDA 13.5 or newer assembler, but the ABI can be "
"proven today. The example's host program stands in for the Tile compiler:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:414
msgid "cargo oxide run tilelib_relu --emit-nvvm-ir --arch sm_120\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:418
msgid ""
"It reads `tilelib_relu.tileexports.json`, generates `mock/caller.cu` with "
"the prototypes the ABI table predicts and one kernel per export, compiles "
"that with `nvcc` to LTOIR, links it with the Rust LTOIR through nvJitLink, "
"the same two-input link a consumed library gets, and runs one block of 128 "
"threads per export on the GPU. Every result is compared bit for bit:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:424
msgid ""
"PASS relu: 128 elements through `relu_impl`, relu of a ramp from -4 to 4\n"
"PASS scale_in_place: 128 elements through `scale_in_place_impl`, the ramp "
"scaled in place by 0.5\n"
"PASS warp_sum: 128 elements through `warp_sum_impl`, 32-lane sums over "
"0..128, every lane holding its warp's total\n"
"SUCCESS: 3/3 tile exports verified through the native ABI\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/tile-libraries.md:431
msgid ""
"The prototypes in `mock/caller.cu` are derived from the descriptions, not "
"written by hand, so the generated file is the ABI as the tooling understands "
"it. Without `nvcc`, or on a GPU that does not match `--arch`, the program "
"prints `skipping:` with the reason and exits 0."
msgstr ""
